r in the  first place  thus other than yuji or megomi she has  already  reached her goal and as a result her  desire has now shifted from getting to  tokyo to preserving the life she has  wished for and has now gained  and the way she approaches it is pretty  much the same as  gojol strength when you're strong you  can shape the world around you  do what you want and keep those safe  that matter  noboda thus constantly balances two  personas  her girly feminine side that is  incredibly important to her  as well as being a strong sorcerer and  woman this is  wonderfully symbolized i think by her  weapon a hammer with a pink heart on it  now this duality of her character that i  personally find incredibly cool and well  executed  is explored in great detail during her  fight with  momo  umo holds the position that women in the  g2 world have to be  perfect in order to have a place in  society perfect  of course in the most conservative and  rigid sense of the term  now i think you'll agree that kugisaki  standing up against this idea is a very  important and  impressive move yes women can be both  strong and beautiful and you shouldn't  let your life be determined by what  society says  life isn't a job and you should be able  to be who you are now the thing that  nobody doesn't consider however  is why momo thinks so in the first place  momo has experienced all the unfairness  and harsh expectations that her and the  women around her have faced in the juju  to society  she didn't come up with that view by  herself it's the conclusion she has  drawn personally  from all the suffering and struggle  associated with being a female sorcerer  but  nobody doesn't get that no not only  doesn't she get it  she doesn't want to get it when momo  tries to explain her thoughts  this is her actual reaction to that  [Music]  not having any empathy is the major  flaw of her character just like gojo  kugisaki is  extremely convinced of herself and her  worldview while most other char